<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Anhalt-Zerbst</h1><p class="st-meta">1603 CE – 1793 CE · capital Zerbst</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small Anhalt principality on the Elbe best remembered as the birthplace dynasty of Catherine the Great of Russia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Rudolf, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-ernest-prince-of-anhalt">Joachim Ernst</a>, received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zerbst">Zerbst</a> in the 1603 partition of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-anhalt">Anhalt</a>. The town, north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, had been a prosperous brewing centre known for its beer, and the line included the lordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jever">Jever</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a>, inherited in 1667.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The principality was Lutheran in its later period and modestly governed from a baroque palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zerbst">Zerbst</a>. Its most famous member was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Sophie Auguste Friederike</a>, daughter of Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-august-prince-of-anhalt-zerbst">Christian August</a>, who married the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iii-of-russia">Peter III</a> and became Empress Catherine II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Her brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-august-prince-of-anhalt-zerbst">Friedrich August</a>, the last prince, lived mostly abroad and hired out soldiers to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-august-prince-of-anhalt-zerbst">Friedrich August</a> died without heirs in 1793. The principality was divided among the other <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-anhalt">Anhalt</a> lines, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernburg">Bernburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kothen">Köthen</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jever">Jever</a> passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a> and so to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-anhalt-zerbst.jpg" alt="Principality of Anhalt-Zerbst" width="320" height="167"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Anhalt_(1747-1793).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1603 CE – 1793 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Zerbst</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Rudolf, Christian August, Friedrich August</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Anhalt-Zerbst</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1698&amp;focus=principality-of-anhalt-zerbst" aria-label="Principality of Anhalt-Zerbst on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4952 382 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Anhalt-Zerbst lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5272 638L5276 641M5272 638L5229 694M5272 638L5176 592"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5276 641h0M5229 694h0M5176 592h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5272 638h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5272 638h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Zerbst. <a href="/atlas?year=1698&amp;focus=principality-of-anhalt-zerbst">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
